*Abstract.* When a robot revisits a remembered object, an occluder can hide both the object and the evidence that it was removed. We audit decisions to keep or delete a memory by rendering the same location with the target retained and removed. Three two-image verifiers received 1,280 blinded queries from 16 fresh layouts in Isaac Sim and MuJoCo. After a 6 cm camera shift and 3° yaw, dense geometric registration increased correct removals with the blocker parked from 67/80 to 76/80 in Isaac and from 63/80 to 78/80 in MuJoCo. It also introduced two false removals on MuJoCo shelves. A zero-shot registered vision-language model recognized 80/80 and 79/80 clear removals, respectively, and gave 51 false removal decisions on retained-object queries across the two engines. At the same 88 fully hidden positions, MuJoCo produced identical inputs across opposite target states and Isaac produced unequal images. Homography, dense geometry and the registered language model committed on zero, one and four of the 88 identical pairs. Every observed commitment was REMOVED, yielding one correct and one false removal per pair. Measuring useful decisions alongside these directly witnessed errors exposes a tradeoff that clear-removal accuracy alone obscures.
